	<h2>Faiths of the Crusades</h2>
	<p>The crusades against the demons of the Abyss are fundamentally a faith-driven conflict between the forces and
		agents of the divine and profane. While faith in a deity is not required, it is highly recommended, and many of the
		allies and resources encountered in this campaign have ties to the deities of Golarion, even if their relationship
		is rather complicated. The major faiths featured in this campaign can be grouped as follows:</p>
	<ul style="list-style-type:circle">
		<li style="list-style-type: none;"><b>Click to Reveal the Faiths of the Crusades:</b></li>
		<li style="list-style-type: none; display: none;"><b>Click to Apostatize the Faiths of the Crusades:</b></li>
		<li style="display: none;"><b>Crusading Faiths:</b> <i>(Abadar, Aroden, Asmodeus, Iomedae, Sarenrae, Shelyn,
				Torag)</i><br>
			These faiths and churches actively send crusaders and paladins to fight the Abyss, and are the most appropriate
			choices of faith for an adventurer. The crusade is championed primarily by Iomedae and her forces, with the
			<i>Calling</i> of Queen Galifrey to lead Mendev and petition the launch of the first crusade being one of
			Iomedae's first acts as the Inheritor. Shortly thereafter, she called the Hand of the Inheritor to be her herald
			(formerly the Hand of Vengeance and herald of Ragathiel) to lead the Pyralisia, Terendelev, and her host of angels
			in personally defending Golarion from the swarm of Deskari, as Aroden had done in the flesh three hundred years
			prior. The faithful of Abadar, Sarenrae, Shelyn, and Torag also contribute funds and forces, and form the backbone
			of the logistics, restoration, redemption, and engineering aspects of the crusades. Those who still follow Aroden
			are generally looked at with a mixture of derision and pity, taking up the banner but not the name of the
			inheritor and clinging to a dead god out of what is thought to be stubbornness, foolishness, or a misguided
			dedication to tradition. Meanwhile, the devil-worshipping church of Asmodeus takes great care in sending paladins,
			hellknights, and inquisitors alike to contain the demonic threat, and while their skill and zeal are appreciated,
			they are rarely fully accepted or trusted by the other crusaders.
		</li>
		<li style="display: none;"><b>Sarkorian Faiths:</b> <i>(Desna, Gorum, Gozreh, Lamashtu, Pharasma, Pulura)</i><br>
			An Ustalavan scholar once pejoratively referred to the faiths of the northern Sarkorian peoples as the worship of
			"a hundred hungry gods," deriding their fragmented polytheistic traditions, but this statement failed both to
			properly innumerate the vastness of their pantheon or comprehend the complexity of their worship. Faith and
			practices varied from clan to clan and covered the gamut from deities to concepts, including the veneration of
			ancestors, outsiders, fae, and spirits of all kinds. Each clan adopted personal patrons for the practices of their
			druidism, shamanism, god-calling, and witchcraft, with these patrons often granting supernatural powers. However,
			the most prominent of these faiths still make for good patron deities for adventurers, as they are the most
			involved with local and historical peoples and places, and have a vested interest in the wounded lands. Desna is
			particularly prominent, being responsible for the hope and dreams of all people, and features heavily in this
			campaign. Followers of Gorum seek warfare, and there are few wars as promising of relentless battles as crusades.
			Pharasma as the goddess of death and fate is extremely interested in preserving the river of souls, though she
			does not ultimately care whether souls are bound for either Heaven or the Pitt. Pulura is the goddess of the north
			star and the Aurora, and the house of her worship is lost in the wounded lands, with her faithful eager to recover
			it. Meanwhile Gozreh and the green faith both feature prominently among the druidic traditions of the land, as
			does Lamashtu's interest in the particular beasts unique to this frontier.</li>
		<li style="display: none;"><b>Other Involved Faiths:</b> <i>(Cayden Cailean, Erastil, Ragathiel)</i><br>
			These religions hold a prominent and involved role amongst the crusades, even if their churches do not directly
			send money and troops in support, and these are a pretty good choice for adventurers, particularly those who
			explore the Worldwound for reasons other than duty. Ragathiel was an early and committed party to the crusades,
			granting his herald to Iomedae to further aid the war effort, but he lacks both reach and influence to remain
			prominent, and his followers tend to fight recklessly to the death rather than coordinating a strong resistance.
			The faithful from among Cayden Cailean and Erastil similarly make up fair portion of crusaders, but those that
			join do so out of personal conviction rather than a religious mandate.</li>
		<li style="display: none;"><b>Demonic Faith:</b> <i>(Baphomet, Deskari, Nocticula, Lamashtu)</i><br>
			While demonic faiths are generally not appropriate for adventurers to worship, these are the most prominent demon
			lords involved in the war against the Mendevian Crusaders. Deskari is the most prominent and dangerous, with his
			cultists spreading his faith swiftly through the superstitious and polytheistic faiths of Sarkoris hundreds of
			years before the opening of the Worldwound, and amassing enough power to manifest an avatar physically on Golarion
			at the lake of Mists and Veils, only to be slain by the ascendent hand of Aroden himself. Since then, he continued
			to corrupt the lands and people here, waiting until the death of Aroden to sweep across Golarion in the form of a
			demonic plague, kicking off the first crusade. Decades later, Baphomet took interest in these affairs, perhaps
			through an unholy alliance, and he set his faithful to infiltrate, corrupt, and mislead crusaders, leading to the
			witch hunts of the third crusade. Lamashtu, mother of monsters, meanwhile nurtures the corrupting nature of the
			Worldwound, lovingly birthing new abominations, with her feral worshippers often seeking both her power and
			protection. Nocticula meanwhile loves engaging in the politics and shadow games of mortals, and the Worldwound
			gives her a chance to play the same game with demons and gods, as well as their easily seduced followers. Her
			spies pry secrets from all sides and use them with surgical precision to seed as much discord, death, and
			destruction as possible. While other demons also have worshippers of their own, these demon lords are at the top
			of their food chains.</li>
	</ul>
</section>